Second candidate fix for the memory-pressure freeze (see
docs/load-test-modules.md "The real root cause, confirmed" and its
2026-09-21 correction, and kernel/patches/0007-backport-genpd-psci-osi-
power-unknown.patch's status note). Patch 0007 (backported unmerged
upstream genpd/cpuidle-psci OSI-mode fix) was tested on real hardware
and did NOT stop the freeze - it fixes genpd's initial boot-time power-
state bookkeeping under OSI mode, not a live idle-state-transition race.

This patch targets that live race directly instead. Downstream's own
LPM driver (references/gts7l/drivers/cpuidle/lpm-levels.c,
cluster_configure()) explicitly checks is_IPI_pending() before allowing
a CPU cluster to collapse into its deepest shared idle state
(equivalent to cluster_sleep_0 here). Mainline's generic PSCI/genpd
cpuidle governor has no equivalent guard anywhere in this path. This is
a direct structural match for the observed failure: lru_add_drain_all()
(called by khugepaged and directly by memory reclaim under pressure)
dispatches an IPI to every CPU, and if a cluster idle-state collapse is
in flight at that exact moment with no such guard, a CPU can end up in
a state that never answers anything again - not even an NMI (confirmed
twice independently: CPU 5 in an earlier crash, CPU 6 in a later one,
both under this exact scenario).

Rather than trying to reimplement that missing guard in mainline's
generic cpuidle-psci path (invasive, easy to get subtly wrong), this
just removes the opportunity for the race outright: dropping
domain-idle-states from the cluster power domain means CPUs stay in
their shallower per-CPU idle states (little_cpu_sleep_0/
big_cpu_sleep_0) and the shared cluster domain is never asked to
collapse at all. Matches the remedy pattern used for the same bug class
on Snapdragon X Elite.

Cost: gives up the deepest shared-cluster low-power state
(cluster_sleep_0 - entry-latency-us 3264, exit-latency-us 6562,
min-residency-us 9987) system-wide. Per-CPU idle states are unaffected.
Expect a battery-life regression under light/idle load in exchange for
not freezing under memory pressure; acceptable for a debug build, worth
reassessing once (or if) the actual missing-guard fix is implemented in
mainline instead.

Status: built, flashed, and tested on real hardware (2026-09-21).
RESULT: partial fix.

Confirmed via /sys/kernel/debug/pm_genpd/pm_genpd_summary that
power-domain-cpu-cluster0 now stays "on" permanently (never collapses),
as intended.

- `stress-ng --vm 1 --vm-bytes 1500M --vm-keep --timeout 30s` - the
  exact repro that reliably froze every prior kernel this whole
  investigation (including with patch 0007 alone) - now completes
  cleanly. Ran it once cleanly to completion; host-side reachability
  monitor showed continuous OK with zero UNREACHABLE entries throughout.
  Device fully responsive afterward (`uptime`, `free -h` both normal).
  This is the first time in the whole investigation this exact repro
  has NOT frozen the device.

- `stress-ng --vm 1 --vm-bytes 3G --vm-keep --timeout 30s` (a harder
  repro, also previously reliable, requesting more than the device's
  available memory with zero swap configured) - STILL froze. Host
  monitor went UNREACHABLE at 2026-09-21T13:53:07-03:00 and stayed
  that way; device did not recover after 30+ more seconds (hard freeze,
  not just thrashing). Owner rebooted.

So this fix genuinely narrows the bug (removes at least one real path
into it) but does not eliminate it. Two live hypotheses for the
remaining 3G-only freeze: (1) a second, distinct failure mode specific
to real OOM-killer pressure (do_exit teardown / oom_reaper / TLB
shootdowns under actual memory exhaustion, as opposed to the milder
lru_add_drain_all()-driven reclaim path that the 1.46GB case exercises)
that doesn't go through the cluster domain at all; or (2) the same
is_IPI_pending()-shaped race still applies at the *per-CPU* idle-state
level (little_cpu_sleep_0/big_cpu_sleep_0, still enabled - only the
cluster-level domain-idle-state was removed by this patch), and heavier
IPI traffic under real OOM pressure still manages to hit it there.
Follow-up (2026-09-21, diagnostic only, not committed here - see below):
also disabled the per-CPU idle states (little_cpu_sleep_0/
big_cpu_sleep_0, referenced from cpu_pd0-7) to test hypothesis (2).
RESULT: ruled out. With every CPU idle state disabled device-wide
(cluster AND all 8 per-CPU domains - CPUs can only WFI, no real
power-collapse anywhere in the system), the 3G repro still froze hard
(host monitor UNREACHABLE from 2026-09-21T14:10:26-03:00, no recovery).
This is not a cpuidle/PSCI idle-state race at any level - hypothesis
(1), a distinct failure specific to real OOM-killer pressure unrelated
to CPU power states, is now the only live explanation. The per-CPU
idle-state disable was diagnostic-only (real battery cost, no benefit)
and has been reverted; only this patch's cluster-level change is kept.

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250.dtsi
index 707672041..fa3f66d3e 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250.dtsi
@@ -745,7 +745,18 @@ cpu_pd7: power-domain-cpu7 {

 		cluster_pd: power-domain-cpu-cluster0 {
 			#power-domain-cells = <0>;
-			domain-idle-states = <&cluster_sleep_0>;
+			/*
+			 * domain-idle-states = <&cluster_sleep_0>; intentionally
+			 * removed: mainline's cpuidle-psci genpd governor has no
+			 * equivalent to downstream lpm-levels.c's is_IPI_pending()
+			 * guard before collapsing this shared cluster state, and an
+			 * IPI (e.g. from lru_add_drain_all() under memory pressure)
+			 * racing a collapse in flight leaves a CPU permanently
+			 * unresponsive, even to NMI. Without domain-idle-states here,
+			 * CPUs stay in their per-CPU idle states and the cluster
+			 * domain is never asked to collapse. See
+			 * docs/load-test-modules.md (2026-09-21) for the full trail.
+			 */
 		};
 	};
